        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(sdk): add prototype-pollution guard to migration config merge (#103059)",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n- Add `isBlockedObjectKey` guard to `mergeMigrationConfigValue` and `writeMigrationConfigPath` in `src/plugin-sdk/migration.ts`.\n- Reject `__proto__`/`constructor`/`prototype` keys before they reach bracket assignments, matching the guard already used by the sibling config-path writer.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe migration-import config merge helpers had no prototype-pollution guard. A crafted or tampered prior-tool config file imported through `openclaw onboard`'s migration flow that contains a map entry named `__proto__` could trigger prototype pollution. This is the sixth instance of this bug class in the repo; the other five files were already fixed.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- **Why it matters / User impact:** Prototype pollution from imported config files is prevented. The guard is consistent with the sibling `parseConfigPath`/`setConfigValueAtPath` that already rejects blocked keys.\n- **What did NOT change:** Normal config migration continues to work. Only `__proto__`/`constructor`/`prototype` keys are now silently dropped.\n- **Architecture / source-of-truth check:** Uses the same `isBlockedObjectKey` from `src/infra/prototype-keys.js` that protects the sibling config-path writer.\n\n## Origin / follow-up\n\nFixes #103059. The issue reports that migration config merge has no prototype-pollution guard, unlike the sibling config-path writer.\n\n## Competition / linked PR analysis\n\nNo overlapping PRs found.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Source inspection confirms `isBlockedObjectKey` is now called in both `mergeMigrationConfigValue` and `writeMigrationConfigPath`.\n- The same guard is used in `src/config/config-paths.ts:42` (`parseConfigPath`).\n- Format clean (oxfmt).\n\nFixes #103059\n",
        "branch": "fix/migration-prototype-pollution-guard",
